1
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:08,640
Could the knowledge of a language model
be a list of rules that you can read,

2
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,570
at a fraction of the power?

3
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:14,200
In ten studies, I measured
how far Tsetlin machines,

4
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,090
which learn logical clauses,
get on language.

5
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:20,750
No model built here is called
a large language model.

6
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:25,580
The clause form comes from
my supervisor's project proposal,

7
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:26,870
which is unpublished.

8
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,730
Inside a group, single words
are joined by a logical OR.

9
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:32,970
The groups are joined by a logical AND.

10
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:35,330
The clause fires
when every group finds

11
00:00:35,410 --> 00:00:37,980
at least one of its words in the text.

12
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:42,590
An earlier study,
which ended mostly negative,

13
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:45,700
tested the proposal's procedure
with the package it names,

14
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:46,980
the stock model.

15
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:51,400
The latest study keeps the clause form
and replaces the learning rule:

16
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:55,410
one small automaton
for every clause, group and word.

17
00:00:55,570 --> 00:00:59,100
It is a different model,
and the earlier verdicts stand.

18
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:05,070
Test one: artificial worlds
that hide 8 groups of words.

19
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,340
The registered bars are met
on 5 of 5 seeds,

20
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:10,750
with a group recovery of 1.0.

21
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,810
But the rule was designed
on worlds of the same generator.

22
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:21,030
Test two: slide 10 of the proposal,
one clause for a sentence family.

23
00:01:21,110 --> 00:01:23,510
With direct labels
and a different model,

24
00:01:23,590 --> 00:01:26,390
the best single clause
meets the bars of Check 2b

25
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:30,870
on 5 of 5 registered seeds,
on 8 of 20 further seeds,

26
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:34,240
and on 13 of 25 pooled:
about one half.

27
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:37,210
The pair version,
the procedure of the proposal,

28
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:39,700
met none of the bars
on the DEV sentences.

29
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:44,620
Test three: Wikipedia paragraph pairs.

30
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:48,120
On held-back test pairs,
the three rules of the registered test

31
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:51,010
are met, as predicted from DEV:

32
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:55,240
the OR-group rule is above the stock model
in its registered setting

33
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:02,320
by 0.091, 0.101 and 0.098
AUC of the vote sum.

34
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,640
It is not a comparison
at matched size or cost,

35
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:10,020
so it does not tell
what makes the difference,

36
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:13,090
and the test has no rule
against word overlap.

37
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:19,350
The vote sum minus PPMI cosine,
a baseline built from word counts,

38
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:23,430
has its interval below zero
on all three kinds of pairs.

39
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:28,140
Minus logistic regression, the interval
is below zero on the two kinds

40
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,310
where that baseline is above chance.

41
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:34,700
The cost: the nine models
of the design stage

42
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:37,830
have a median size
of about 22 kilobytes.

43
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:44,870
Each registered test run took
192 to 230 seconds on one CPU thread.

44
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:47,580
The energy is bounded, not measured.

45
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:52,340
In the one measured comparison
of training energy, indicative only,

46
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:54,100
the Tsetlin machine language model

47
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:58,990
used about 8.5 times the energy
of a small transformer, and scored worse.

48
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:04,180
A readable agent,
with no neural network at any stage,

49
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:06,990
chooses the next action
in logged dialogues.

50
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:12,480
Within the registered margin of 3 points,
it is not worse than a prompted local LLM

51
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:18,410
on three test sets; on one of them,
on seed 1 only, not on the 5-seed mean.

52
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:23,310
The timings are rough: it decides
in milliseconds on one CPU thread,

53
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:26,990
against seconds on a GPU
for the LLM.

54
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:31,690
But it is better on no test set,
a fine-tuned transformer is ahead,

55
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:38,570
and TF-IDF logistic regression is level
or ahead, and faster on two of the three.

56
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,080
Code, registrations and results pages
are in the repository.

57
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:48,250
No number of the studies
can be recomputed from a clone alone.

58
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,470
The registered rules were fixed
before their runs.

59
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:54,120
The negative results
are reported like the others.

60
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:57,940
The Tsetlin machine language model,
in a single-seed run,

61
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:01,060
missed its main accuracy goal
against a 5-gram model.

62
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:04,510
And the proposal's own procedure
ended below word overlap.

63
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:09,070
So the opening question stays open:
no power saving was measured.
